\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Federated learning lets a fleet of intrusion detectors train a shared model
without pooling raw traffic, which is attractive for privacy-sensitive IoT
deployments but also opens the aggregation step to poisoning by compromised
participants. We study stealthy backdoor attacks on a federated CNN\,+\,LSTM
network-flow classifier and the Byzantine-robust defenses meant to stop them,
across two recent benchmarks (CICIoT2023 and FLNET2023), under IID and non-IID
client data, spanning 190 attack/defense configurations. Two findings stand
out. First, the backdoor is genuinely stealthy: undefended averaging is
subverted in every configuration (backdoor success rate $0.90$--$1.00$) while
clean accuracy is untouched, so the poisoning is invisible to accuracy
monitoring. Second---and contrary to the usual framing that the danger scales
with the number of attackers---the axis that actually decides whether a defense
holds is the \emph{salience} of the trigger, not the malicious-client fraction.
A conspicuous fixed-pattern trigger is filtered by every similarity-based
defense, whereas low-salience, in-distribution triggers (statistical and
temporal) leave a poisoned update almost collinear with an honest one and defeat
Krum, FLAME, FoolsGold, and our method alike. We also propose \textsc{GradCos}, a
reference-free defense that flags the colluding minority from the pairwise
cosine structure of client updates without any clean server-side data. Over five
seeds at the headline operating point it is the best defense on fixed-pattern
backdoors---tying FLTrust's backdoor rate ($0.004$) while keeping $+0.15$ higher
clean accuracy, and holding where Krum collapses---but it inherits the same blind
spot on low-salience triggers. We trace that blind spot to the shared main-task
gradient masking the backdoor direction, and show that mean-centring the updates
restores detection ($0.11\rightarrow0.99$) and gives a near-complete defense on
the statistical trigger, though only a partial one on temporal. We argue that
reporting clean accuracy alongside
backdoor success is essential, because two baselines buy their low backdoor rate
with a badly degraded model; and that seeds matter, since the strongest sybil
baseline (FoolsGold) swings from a backdoor rate of $0.003$ to $0.926$ across
seeds on the same cell. We release the full pipeline for reproduction.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
federated learning, backdoor attacks, intrusion detection, Byzantine-robust
aggregation, IoT security, poisoning.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Intrusion detection on IoT and edge networks is a natural fit for federated
learning (FL). The traffic that a detector needs is exactly the traffic that
operators are least willing to centralize, and FL~\cite{mcmahan2017,kairouz2021}
keeps it on the device: each participant trains locally and only shares model updates, which
a server averages into a global detector. The catch is that the averaging step
trusts whoever shows up. A participant that has been compromised---a common
assumption in a large, heterogeneous IoT fleet---can submit crafted updates that
steer the shared model. Among poisoning attacks, \emph{backdoors} are the most
insidious: rather than degrade the model outright, they teach it a hidden rule
(``traffic carrying pattern $\tau$ is benign'') while leaving normal behaviour
intact~\cite{bagdasaryan2020,xie2020}. In an intrusion detector this means an
attacker can wave their own attack traffic past the model at will, and nothing
in the aggregate accuracy gives it away.

A sizeable literature answers this with Byzantine-robust aggregation. Krum picks
the update sitting in the densest neighbourhood~\cite{blanchard2017}; FLTrust
scores each update against a direction the server derives from a small clean
dataset~\cite{cao2021fltrust}; FLAME clusters updates, clips their norms, and
adds noise~\cite{nguyen2022flame}. These methods are usually evaluated on image
benchmarks with vivid pixel-patch triggers, and they are usually reported by
their backdoor success rate alone. Neither habit transfers cleanly to
intrusion detection, where the ``trigger'' is a perturbation of tabular flow
statistics that must itself stay plausible, and where a defense that flattens the
model to suppress a backdoor has quietly broken the detector it was protecting.

This paper takes the FL intrusion-detection setting on its own terms. We build a
federated CNN\,+\,LSTM flow classifier and attack it with three families of
in-distribution triggers on network-flow features, sweeping malicious-client
fraction and poison rate, and we defend it with five established aggregators plus
a new one. Working through CICIoT2023 and FLNET2023, IID and non-IID---with the
headline comparison repeated over five seeds---we reach a picture that is more
nuanced than ``defense $X$ wins.'' Our contributions:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{A systematic FL-IDS backdoor study} across two 2023 benchmarks,
three trigger designs, three malicious ratios, three poison rates, and six
aggregation rules (FedAvg, Krum, FLTrust, FLAME, FoolsGold, and GradCos),
reporting clean accuracy and macro-F1 together with backdoor success and
detection rates---with the headline operating point over five seeds
(mean\,$\pm$\,s.d.)---so that the cost \emph{and} the stability of each defense is
visible.

\item \textbf{The main empirical finding:} what governs robustness is trigger
salience, not the number of attackers. Conspicuous fixed-pattern triggers are
neutralized by every similarity-based defense even at $30\%$ malicious clients,
while low-salience statistical and temporal triggers---perturbations of about
$1.5$--$2\sigma$ that stay inside the honest data distribution---defeat all of
them, because the poisoned update's \emph{direction} barely departs from an
honest one and the cosine signal these defenses rely on vanishes.

\item \textbf{\textsc{GradCos}}, a reference-free aggregator that identifies the
colluding minority from the pairwise cosine structure of the updates, needing no
clean server dataset (unlike FLTrust) and no manual estimate of the number of
attackers (unlike Krum). Over five seeds it is the best fixed-trigger defense---
tying FLTrust's backdoor rate while keeping FedAvg-level clean accuracy that
FLTrust and FLAME sacrifice---holds where Krum collapses, and is strongest at the
non-IID operating point we test. It inherits the shared low-salience blind spot,
but we \emph{diagnose} that blind spot (the shared task gradient masks the
backdoor direction) and show a simple mean-centred variant that restores
detection to $0.99$ and near-fully defends the statistical trigger---a partial
fix we report with its limits (temporal stays only partly contained).

\item \textbf{A ``no free lunch'' caution and a variance caution:} on CICIoT2023
the baselines with the lowest backdoor rates, FLAME and FLTrust, also have the
worst clean models (accuracy $0.50$ and $0.56$ versus $0.70$), so part of their
robustness is a weakened detector; and the strongest sybil baseline, FoolsGold,
is so seed-dependent (fixed-trigger BSR $0.003$--$0.926$) that a single-seed
number would badly mislead. Both distinctions are invisible to a
backdoor-success-only, single-seed comparison.
\end{itemize}

We release the code, configurations, and per-round logs so the grid can be
regenerated with a single command.

\section{Related Work}

\textbf{Backdoors in federated learning.} Bagdasaryan et
al.~\cite{bagdasaryan2020} showed that model-replacement lets a single client
implant a durable backdoor; Xie et al.~\cite{xie2020} distributed the trigger
across colluding clients (DBA) to make each update look more benign. Wang et
al.~\cite{wang2020tails} argued backdoors on rare sub-populations are especially
hard to remove, while Sun et al.~\cite{sun2019} found that norm clipping and
noise blunt many backdoors---foreshadowing FLAME. Most of this work uses image
classification with patch triggers. Intrusion detection differs in that the
feature space is tabular flow statistics and the trigger must remain a
physically plausible flow, which is the regime we study.

\textbf{Byzantine-robust aggregation.} Krum and Multi-Krum~\cite{blanchard2017}
and coordinate-wise median / trimmed mean~\cite{yin2018} give distance- or
order-statistic-based robustness. FLTrust~\cite{cao2021fltrust} adds a trusted
root dataset to anchor a reference direction. FoolsGold~\cite{fung2020foolsgold}
targets sybil collusion by penalizing clients whose updates are too similar to
each other---the opposite reading of the same cosine signal we use. FLAME
~\cite{nguyen2022flame} and DeepSight~\cite{rieger2022deepsight} combine
clustering with clipping/filtering. Shejwalkar and
Houmansadr~\cite{shejwalkar2021} showed that many of these defenses fall to
optimized attacks, which is consistent with our finding that a low-salience
trigger slips under the similarity radar.

\textbf{Federated IDS.} Federated detectors have been built on modern IoT
benchmarks such as CICIoT2023~\cite{neto2023ciciot} and the federated traffic
benchmark FLNET2023~\cite{flnet2023}. Comparatively little of this work stress-
tests the aggregation layer against stealthy backdoors with clean-accuracy
accounting, which is the gap this paper addresses.

\section{Threat Model and System Setup}

\textbf{Federated detector.} $N=20$ clients hold private shards of a
network-flow dataset and train a shared classifier for $R=100$ synchronous
rounds at full participation, one local epoch per round (batch size $256$, Adam,
learning rate $10^{-3}$). The model treats the $F$-dimensional standardized
feature vector as a length-$F$, single-channel sequence: a Conv1D layer
($64$ filters, kernel $3$, ReLU, BatchNorm) extracts local feature
interactions, an LSTM ($128$ hidden units) reads the resulting sequence, and a
dense head ($64$ units, dropout $0.3$) with softmax produces the class
posterior. Aggregation is performed by genuine Flower~\cite{beutel2020flower}
\texttt{Strategy} objects driven by an in-process round loop, so every defense
receives real per-client \texttt{FitRes} updates.

\textbf{Adversary.} A fraction $m\in\{0.1,0.2,0.3\}$ of clients is malicious and
colludes on a single backdoor objective: make trigger-carrying attack traffic be
classified as benign. Each malicious client poisons a fraction
$p\in\{0.05,0.1,0.2\}$ of its \emph{attack} samples by stamping the trigger on
them and relabelling them benign; it then trains normally. The adversary knows
the model architecture and the trigger but does not control the server or honest
clients. This is a data-poisoning (``simple'') backdoor; we also implement a
norm-matching (\emph{constrained}) variant and a distributed (DBA) variant, but
report the simple variant, which is the one swept at scale.

\textbf{Server capability.} The server sees only the model updates. It has no
access to raw client data. FLTrust is the one exception we include on purpose:
it is granted a small class-balanced clean root set ($\approx$300 samples), so
its results should be read as an upper bound that assumes a resource other
defenses do without.

\textbf{Metrics.} We report clean accuracy (CA) and macro-F1 on non-triggered
traffic; backdoor success rate (BSR), the fraction of triggered attack samples
called benign; and, for defenses that flag clients, detection rate (DR) and
false-positive rate (FPR) against the known malicious set. A defense is good only
if it drives BSR down \emph{while} holding CA up; we therefore never report BSR
in isolation.

\section{Backdoor Trigger Design}
\label{sec:triggers}

Because the classifier consumes standardized features, we define triggers in
normalized space, i.e.\ in units of standard deviations, so that train-time
poisoning and test-time evaluation are consistent by construction. The trigger
stamps a fixed pattern onto a small set of feature columns; Table~\ref{tab:trig}
lists the three designs. The design choice that matters is magnitude. An
all-same-sign, extreme pattern ($\geq 4\sigma$) lands far outside the data
distribution, where the \emph{clean} model already predicts benign---so the
``backdoor'' succeeds with no poisoning at all. That is an out-of-distribution
artifact, not a learned backdoor. We therefore use \emph{mixed-sign}, moderate
($\approx 1.5$--$2\sigma$) patterns verified to have a clean-model BSR near zero,
so the association must actually be learned from poisoned updates, and the
triggered flow stays in-distribution and hence stealthy. The same three
themes---flag/header features, volume/dispersion features, and
inter-arrival/count features---are expressed in each dataset's own feature
schema.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Trigger families (values in units of $\sigma$; CICIoT2023 schema).}
\label{tab:trig}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Trigger & Semantics & Example features / values \\
\midrule
Fixed & header \& TCP flags & TTL$=+2$, hdr\_len$=-1.5$, syn$=+2$ \\
Statistical & volume / dispersion & Rate$=-1.5$, Std$=+2$, Tot\,size$=+1.5$ \\
Temporal & inter-arrival / counts & IAT$=+2$, Number$=+1.5$, size$=-1.5$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Defenses}

We evaluate five aggregators from the literature and one new one. All subclass
FedAvg and differ only in \texttt{aggregate\_fit}.

\textbf{FedAvg}~\cite{mcmahan2017} is the undefended, example-weighted mean---the
attack's baseline.

\textbf{Krum / Multi-Krum}~\cite{blanchard2017} scores each update by the sum of
squared distances to its $n-f-2$ nearest neighbours and keeps the update(s) in
the densest region; we use Multi-Krum with $f$ clamped to what the cohort size
supports.

\textbf{FLTrust}~\cite{cao2021fltrust} trains the current global model one step
on the server's clean root set to get a trusted direction $g_0$, weights each
client update by $\mathrm{ReLU}(\cos(g_i,g_0))$, rescales it to $\|g_0\|$, and
averages. Updates pointing away from $g_0$ get zero weight.

\textbf{FLAME}~\cite{nguyen2022flame} clusters updates by cosine distance
(HDBSCAN), keeps the majority cluster, clips admitted updates to their median
norm, and adds Gaussian noise. We fixed a subtle bug that had invalidated our
early FLAME runs: the differential-privacy noise was reaching the BatchNorm
\texttt{running\_var} buffers and driving them negative, so eval-mode BatchNorm
computed $\sqrt{\cdot}$ of a negative number and produced NaN predictions. We now
mask the noise to trainable parameters only.

\textbf{FoolsGold}~\cite{fung2020foolsgold} is a sybil-resistant aggregator that
reads the same cosine signal in reverse: it accumulates each client's historical
update direction and \emph{down-weights} clients whose histories are too similar
to one another, on the premise that colluding sybils share an objective while
honest clients do not. We add it as a recent, reference-free point of comparison.

\subsection{GradCos (proposed)}

The premise is that honest clients optimize the same objective on
similar-distribution data, so their update \emph{directions} are mutually
well-aligned, whereas a backdoor pulls a client's update toward the
trigger\,$\rightarrow$\,benign association and lowers its cosine similarity to the
honest majority. Crucially the signal is purely inter-client: no clean server
dataset is needed. Let $\delta_i = w_i - w^{(t)}$ be client $i$'s update relative
to the round's global model and $\hat\delta_i = \delta_i/\|\delta_i\|$; the
method works from the pairwise matrix $C_{ij} = \langle
\hat\delta_i,\hat\delta_j\rangle$.

The naive reading---flag clients whose \emph{mean} similarity to the cohort is a
robust-$z$ below the median (our \texttt{meansim} variant)---fails under
collusion: when $20$--$30\%$ of clients submit mutually similar backdoor updates,
each attacker's mean similarity is propped up by its fellow attackers and none
looks like a tail outlier. Our default \texttt{clique} variant turns collusion
into a signal instead. Near convergence, honest updates shrink toward noise and
point in diverse directions, so each honest client's \emph{maximum} similarity to
any peer is low; colluding attackers keep injecting the same backdoor direction,
so each has a near-twin and an anomalously high top-similarity. GradCos computes
$s_i=\max_{j\neq i} C_{ij}$, flags clients with $s_i > \mathrm{med}(s) + \kappa\,
1.4826\,\mathrm{MAD}(s)$, and averages the rest. Two guards protect clean
accuracy and low-ratio detection: a homogeneity gate keeps everyone when most
clients have a twin (early training, or a benign IID cohort where all updates
align), and when no clique is found the rule falls back to the mean-similarity
outlier test so an isolated attacker is still caught. A benign-majority cap and a
safety floor ensure at least half the cohort is retained.

\textbf{Residual variant.} The full-vector cosine above is dominated by the shared
main-task gradient, which is exactly why low-salience triggers---whose backdoor
component is small---slip past it (Sec.~\ref{sec:salience}). We therefore add a
\texttt{residual} mode that mean-centres the updates before the cosine test:
$r_i=\delta_i-\bar\delta$ with $\bar\delta=\tfrac1n\sum_j\delta_j$, then runs the
same clique rule on $\hat r_i=r_i/\|r_i\|$. Subtracting the cohort mean removes
the common task direction and leaves the colluders' shared backdoor component
exposed. Sec.~\ref{sec:residual} shows this recovers detection on the very
triggers that defeat the full-vector version.

\section{Experimental Setup}

CICIoT2023~\cite{neto2023ciciot} is used in its 8-class grouped form ($39$
features), stratified-subsampled to $400$k training and $100$k test rows to keep
the $20$-client, $100$-round grid tractable; FLNET2023~\cite{flnet2023} uses the
CICFlowMeter feature schema with category labels (DDoS/DoS/Infiltration/Web/
Normal). Features are standardized with a scaler fit on the training split.
Clients are partitioned IID, or non-IID via a Dirichlet split ($\alpha=0.5$). The
CICIoT2023 IID breadth grid is $5\text{ defenses} \times (1\text{ clean} +
3\text{ triggers} \times 3\text{ ratios} \times 3\text{ poison rates}) = 140$
single-seed cells; FLNET2023 is a one-at-a-time sweep ($40$ cells) and the
non-IID slice is one configuration across the five defenses ($10$ cells).
Separately, the \textbf{headline operating point} ($m{=}0.2$, $p{=}0.1$) is run
over \textbf{five seeds} (42--46) for all three triggers and \emph{six} defenses
---the five above plus FoolsGold~\cite{fung2020foolsgold}---and reported as
mean~$\pm$~s.d.\ (Table~\ref{tab:main}). Where a single-seed breadth cell sits at
a seed-sensitive threshold we keep the worst-case draw rather than the best
(Sec.~\ref{sec:seed}). Every reported number is machine-generated from the result
files; nothing is hand-entered.

\section{Results}

\subsection{The attack is stealthy}

Under undefended FedAvg the backdoor succeeds everywhere. At the headline
operating point ($m{=}0.2$, $p{=}0.1$), averaged over five seeds, FedAvg's BSR is
$0.97\pm0.01$ on the fixed trigger and $1.00$ on both low-salience triggers
(Table~\ref{tab:main}), while its clean accuracy ($0.702$) is indistinguishable
from the no-attack model. Across the broader single-seed grid the picture is the
same---every one of the $27$ attack cells has BSR $\geq 0.90$. In other words the
poisoning leaves no accuracy footprint, which is precisely what makes it
dangerous: an operator watching validation accuracy sees a healthy detector. This
confirms the attack premise and motivates aggregation-level defenses rather than
accuracy monitoring.

