\subsection{Trigger salience decides the outcome}
\label{sec:salience}

The clearest signal in the whole study is in the columns of
Table~\ref{tab:main} and in Fig.~\ref{fig:multiseed}. Group the results not by
attacker count but by trigger:

\begin{itemize}
\item On the \emph{fixed} trigger the similarity- and reference-based defenses
work well: GradCos $0.004\pm0.003$ and FLTrust $0.004\pm0.000$ are lowest, FLAME
$0.010$, then Krum $0.948$ (which has already collapsed at $20\%$ malicious,
Sec.~\ref{sec:collusion}) and FoolsGold $0.369$, against FedAvg's $0.968$. Only
GradCos reaches the low BSR \emph{and} keeps FedAvg-level clean accuracy.
\item On the \emph{statistical} and \emph{temporal} triggers the similarity-based
defenses fail almost completely. GradCos, Krum, and FoolsGold all sit at $1.00$;
FLAME at $0.86$/$0.86$. The lone exception is FLTrust on statistical
($0.007$)---and it uses its clean root direction, not inter-client similarity;
even FLTrust is beaten by the temporal trigger ($0.78\pm0.15$).
\end{itemize}

The reason is mechanical. The fixed trigger stamps header/flag features that few
benign flows exercise, so learning it bends a poisoned client's update in a
direction honest clients never take---a large cosine departure that any of these
filters can see. The statistical and temporal triggers perturb volume, dispersion
and timing features by only $1.5$--$2\sigma$ and stay inside the honest data
distribution; the poisoned update is then nearly collinear with an honest one,
and the cosine signal that Krum, FLAME, FoolsGold, and GradCos all depend on
simply is not there. Fig.~\ref{fig:detection} makes this explicit for GradCos:
its malicious-client detection rate is $0.80$ on the fixed trigger but collapses
to $0.19$ and $0.12$ on the statistical and temporal triggers, and BSR tracks
that collapse. The malicious-client fraction is a secondary axis; the primary one
is how far the trigger pushes the update off the honest manifold. That FoolsGold
---a defense built precisely to catch colluding sybils by their cosine
similarity---also fails here, and is besides wildly seed-dependent on the fixed
trigger (BSR range $0.003$--$0.926$; Table~\ref{tab:main}), underlines that the
blind spot is a property of the \emph{signal}, not of any one defense's tuning.

