\subsection{Recovering detection: the residual variant}
\label{sec:residual}

If the blind spot is caused by the shared main-task gradient drowning the
backdoor component, mean-centring the updates before the cosine test should
expose it. It does---for detection. Table~\ref{tab:residual} compares the default
\texttt{clique} GradCos with the \texttt{residual} variant at the headline point
over five seeds. On both low-salience triggers the residual variant lifts
GradCos's malicious-client detection rate from $\approx0.11$ to $\approx0.99$: the
colluders, invisible in the full update, stand out clearly once the common task
direction is removed. This is direct evidence that the blind spot is a property
of the \emph{signal} the defenses read, not an intrinsic limit---strip the right
component and the collusion is detectable.

Detection, however, does not translate into suppression equally. On the
\emph{statistical} trigger the recovered detection is enough: BSR falls from
$1.000$ to $0.085\pm0.131$---a near-complete defense where every other
similarity-based method failed. On the \emph{temporal} trigger the backdoor is
only partly contained (BSR $1.000\rightarrow0.367\pm0.170$) \emph{despite}
detection at $0.99$. The residual BSR there rises in \emph{late} rounds while the
detection rate stays near $1.0$, so it is not explained by a detection lapse; why
near-perfect client detection still leaves a partial temporal backdoor is an open
question we flag rather than resolve. The honest summary is that mean-centring
closes the detection half of the blind spot outright and the suppression half for
the statistical trigger, leaving the temporal trigger as the harder residual
case. We report this as a positive but bounded result; it is a single dataset at
one operating point, and the temporal gap is unexplained.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{GradCos \texttt{clique} vs.\ \texttt{residual} at the headline point
(CICIoT2023, IID, $m{=}0.2$, $p{=}0.1$; mean\,$\pm$\,s.d.\ over 5 seeds).
Mean-centring restores detection (DR) on the low-salience triggers; suppression
(BSR) follows fully for statistical, partly for temporal.}
\label{tab:residual}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\texttt{clique} (default)} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\texttt{residual}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-5}
Trigger & BSR & DR & BSR & DR \\
\midrule
Fixed       & $0.004$ & $0.97$ & $0.003$ & $0.99$ \\
Statistical & $1.000$ & $0.11$ & $\mathbf{0.085}$ & $\mathbf{0.99}$ \\
Temporal    & $1.000$ & $0.14$ & $0.367$ & $\mathbf{0.99}$ \\
