\subsection{Collusion: the malicious-ratio axis}
\label{sec:collusion}

Within the fixed trigger, sweeping the malicious fraction exposes how the
defenses degrade (Table~\ref{tab:ratio}, Fig.~\ref{fig:ratio}; single-seed
breadth sweep). Krum is the
cautionary case: robust at $10\%$ ($0.00$) but collapsing to $0.69$ at $20\%$ and
$0.99$ at $30\%$, because once the malicious set is large enough its members
become each other's nearest neighbours and Krum selects one of them. GradCos,
FLAME and FLTrust hold across the range---GradCos at $0.01/0.16/0.34$, staying
useful where Krum has failed---which is the collusion-robustness the
\texttt{clique} rule was designed for. The residual GradCos rise at $30\%$ comes
from two borderline cells at the lowest poison rate; at $p\geq0.1$ it stays below
$0.08$ even at $30\%$ malicious.

