\subsection{No free lunch: the clean-accuracy cost}
\label{sec:tradeoff}

Reading Table~\ref{tab:main} down the clean-CA column reframes the fixed-trigger
result. FLAME and FLTrust reach a low BSR, but their clean models are the weakest:
CA $0.499\pm0.06$ and $0.556\pm0.06$, macro-F1 $0.334$ and $0.426$, versus
FedAvg's $0.702/0.561$. Their clipping/noising and aggressive reweighting
suppress the backdoor partly by suppressing the model. GradCos, Krum, and
FoolsGold keep FedAvg-level clean accuracy ($0.705$, $0.703$, $0.703$).
Fig.~\ref{fig:tradeoff} plots the two axes together: the desirable corner is high
clean accuracy with low backdoor success, and on the fixed trigger GradCos is the
only defense there---it ties FLTrust's BSR ($0.004$) at $+0.15$ clean accuracy.
This is why we insist on reporting CA alongside BSR---on backdoor success alone,
FLAME and FLTrust look strictly best, but a large part of their advantage is a
broken detector.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\columnwidth]{fig_tradeoff.pdf}
\caption{Clean accuracy vs.\ fixed-trigger backdoor success (CICIoT2023, IID;
single-seed breadth view). Top-left is worst, bottom-right is best. FLAME and
FLTrust reach low BSR only by giving up clean accuracy; GradCos alone combines a
usable model with a suppressed backdoor.}
\label{fig:tradeoff}
\end{figure}

\subsection{FLNET2023}

FLNET2023 is an easier classification task---clean accuracy reaches $0.9997$ and
macro-F1 $0.9996$ for FedAvg, Krum and GradCos---which sharpens the
clean-accuracy point. FLAME and FLTrust again pay for robustness with the model:
their clean macro-F1 falls to $0.57$ and $0.60$ even though this dataset is
near-separable (Table~\ref{tab:flnet}). At the headline operating point
($m=0.2,p=0.1$, fixed trigger) FedAvg is fully backdoored ($0.998$) while Krum,
FLAME and FLTrust suppress it ($0.007$, $0.017$, $0.016$). GradCos fails at this
exact point on the worst-case seed, which we discuss next.

