\subsection{Non-IID}

At the single non-IID (Dirichlet, $\alpha=0.5$) configuration we test---fixed
trigger, $m=0.2,p=0.1$---GradCos is the strongest defense (Table~\ref{tab:noniid}):
BSR $0.004$ at clean accuracy $0.784$, the best of both axes. FedAvg is
backdoored ($0.79$) and, notably, so is Krum ($0.93$): heterogeneous honest data
already spreads updates out, which blunts Krum's distance test and leaves room
for the colluding pair to look central. FLAME and FLTrust again suppress the
backdoor at a large clean-accuracy cost ($0.56$ and $0.50$). This is a single
point and we do not overclaim from it, but it is encouraging that the mechanism
GradCos relies on---attackers agreeing more with each other than honest clients
do---survives non-IID heterogeneity here.

