\subsection{Reproducibility and variance}
\label{sec:seed}

The headline comparison (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Fig.~\ref{fig:multiseed}) is run
over \textbf{5 seeds} (42--46) and reported as mean~$\pm$~s.d.; the broader
ratio/poison grid and the FLNET/non-IID slices remain single-seed and are
labelled as such in their captions. Variance is not uniform, and where it is
large it is itself a finding. Two cases stand out. (i)~At \emph{borderline}
operating points the outcome hinges on the seed: the FLNET GradCos fixed
$m0.2p0.1$ cell gives BSR $\{0.98,0.18,0.008,0.008\}$ across seeds---the round in
which the clique first separates shifts, and once the backdoor is in it stays, so
we retain the worst-case draw there. (ii)~\emph{FoolsGold on the fixed trigger}
has s.d.\ $0.49$ (BSR range $0.003$--$0.926$ over the 5 seeds): it either locks
onto the colluders' shared history early or never does. By contrast GradCos and
FLTrust on fixed, and every defense on the low-salience triggers, have small
s.d.\ ($\leq 0.02$ where BSR is near $0$ or $1$)---the salience finding is not a
seed artifact. The takeaway is that single-seed numbers are untrustworthy exactly
at the operating thresholds that matter, which is why the headline table carries
dispersion rather than a single draw.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}

\textbf{The blind spot is shared, and it is the point.} GradCos is not a universal
winner, and we do not present it as one. Its value is threefold: it removes
FLTrust's clean-data assumption and Krum's need to know the attacker count, it
holds where Krum collapses, and it keeps the clean accuracy FLAME/FLTrust give
up. But on low-salience triggers it fails, and so do Krum and FLAME, for the same
reason---they all read update \emph{direction}, and a stealthy in-distribution
trigger barely moves it. That common failure is arguably the more important
result than any ranking of the defenses: it says the entire family of cosine/
distance filters has a structural blind spot exactly where a competent attacker
would aim. That blind spot, though, is not immovable. Our \texttt{residual}
variant (Sec.~\ref{sec:residual}) shows it stems from the shared main-task
gradient masking the backdoor component: mean-centring the updates restores
detection to $\approx0.99$ on both low-salience triggers and yields a
near-complete defense on the statistical one. It does not fully contain the
temporal trigger---detection is recovered but suppression is only partial, for
reasons we leave open---so we present it as a first, bounded step rather than a
solution. Pairing this reference-free residual filter with a coordinate-wise
magnitude test, and understanding the temporal detection--suppression gap, are
the natural next steps.

\textbf{Scope.} We sweep the simple data-poisoning variant at scale; the
norm-matching and distributed (DBA) variants are implemented but not swept, so
our claims are about direct poisoning. FLNET2023 is a one-at-a-time sweep rather
than the full grid, and the non-IID evidence is a single configuration---enough
to show the mechanism survives heterogeneity, not enough to characterize it. The
CICIoT2023 grouped-task clean accuracy ($\approx0.70$, macro-F1 $\approx0.55$)
reflects a deliberately small $20$-client, $100$-round, $400$k-row budget and is
not a state-of-the-art centralized IDS number; the comparisons across defenses
are internally consistent, which is what the study needs, but the absolute figure
should not be read as a benchmark result. Finally, our detection metrics assume
the malicious set is known for evaluation only; the defenses themselves never see
those labels.

\section{Conclusion}

We studied stealthy backdoors against a federated CNN\,+\,LSTM intrusion detector
and the Byzantine-robust defenses meant to stop them, over 190 configurations on
two 2023 benchmarks. The attack is invisible to accuracy monitoring, and the
factor that decides whether a defense survives is the salience of the trigger,
not the number of attackers: conspicuous triggers are filtered by every
similarity-based defense even at $30\%$ malicious clients, while low-salience
in-distribution triggers defeat all of them because they hardly move the update
direction the defenses inspect. Our reference-free \textsc{GradCos} matches the
strongest baselines on fixed-pattern backdoors without a clean server dataset and
without knowing the attacker count, and preserves the clean accuracy that two
baselines sacrifice---but it inherits the shared blind spot. We release the full
pipeline and per-round logs to support reproduction and to invite work on the
open problem the study surfaces: robust aggregation against low-salience,
in-distribution triggers.

